{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc | sed -n '1330,1540p'; nl -ba src/cljc/yin/vm.cljc | sed -n '515,555p'\"","aggregated_output":"  1330\t   `terminal-resume-outcome` reads it: an outcome the immediate operation\n  1331\t   raises as an error fails the same way when the resume pops it — and a\n  1332\t   waitset diagnostic, an entry the sweep could not poll at all, raises\n  1333\t   there too, before any continuation is restored.\n  1334\t\n  1335\t   The link wait entries `require` adds (yin.vm.linker.md section 7.2) are\n  1336\t   polled first, by the engine rather than the stream sweep: a\n  1337\t   `:link-request` retries its envelope, a `:link-response` advances its\n  1338\t   own kept cursor to the response carrying its id, and an `:install`\n  1339\t   waits on its install child. The install children are then stepped in\n  1340\t   this same round (section 7.3), so a child that halts restores its\n  1341\t   waiters here; nothing about a child runs inside a restore.\"\n  1342\t  [state]\n  1343\t  (let [wait-set (:wait-set state)]\n  1344\t    (if (and (empty? wait-set) (empty? (:installs state)))\n  1345\t      state\n  1346\t      (let [state (poll-links state\n  1347\t                              (filterv link-entry? wait-set)\n  1348\t                              (remove link-entry? wait-set))\n  1349\t            wait-set (:wait-set state)\n  1350\t            streams (filterv (complement link-entry?) wait-set)\n  1351\t            {:keys [woken store], :as result}\n  1352\t            (waitset/check {:waiting streams}\n  1353\t                           waitset-resolver\n  1354\t                           (:resources state))\n  1355\t            v (assoc state\n  1356\t                     :resources store\n  1357\t                     :wait-set (into (:waiting (:waitset result))\n  1358\t                                     (filter link-entry?)\n  1359\t                                     wait-set))]\n  1360\t        (update v\n  1361\t                :ready-queue (fnil into [])\n  1362\t                (make-woken-run-queue-entries v woken))))))\n  1363\t\n  1364\t\n  1365\t(declare handle-effect resume-continuation)\n  1366\t\n  1367\t\n  1368\t(defn run-loop\n  1369\t  \"Generic eval loop with scheduler support. Ready entries are pure data;\n  1370\t   `resume-fn` pops and restores them.\"\n  1371\t  [state active? step-fn resume-fn]\n  1372\t  (loop [v state]\n  1373\t    (let [q (or (:ready-queue v) [])]\n  1374\t      (cond (active? v) (recur (step-fn v))\n  1375\t            (:blocked? v) (let [v' (check-wait-set v)]\n  1376\t                            (if-let [resumed (resume-fn v')]\n  1377\t                              (recur resumed)\n  1378\t                              (telemetry/emit-snapshot v' :blocked)))\n  1379\t            (seq q) (if-let [resumed (resume-fn v)]\n  1380\t                      (recur resumed)\n  1381\t                      v)\n  1382\t            :else (if (:halted? v) (telemetry/emit-snapshot v :halt) v)))))\n  1383\t\n  1384\t\n  1385\t(def ^:private waitset-diagnostics\n  1386\t  \"The statuses `dao.stream.waitset/check` wakes an entry it could not poll\n  1387\t   at all with: an unsupported reason, an unresolvable entry, or an answer —\n  1388\t   or transport — it could not interpret. No park site in `yin.vm` produces\n  1389\t   an entry that earns one, and an entry that earns one must not reach a\n  1390\t   restore function as a value.\"\n  1391\t  #{:dao.stream.waitset/unsupported-reason\n  1392\t    :dao.stream.waitset/unresolved\n  1393\t    :dao.stream.waitset/invalid-answer})\n  1394\t\n  1395\t\n  1396\t(defn- terminal-resume-outcome\n  1397\t  \"The outcome a woken entry resolved with, when it is one the immediate\n  1398\t   path raises as an error. `handle-put` and `handle-next` raise exactly\n  1399\t   the declared error outcomes of their operation, and whether the first\n  1400\t   attempt blocked must not change that. An outcome outside this contract\n  1401\t   version is the shaped refusal on the immediate path, so it is the\n  1402\t   shaped refusal here too, never a raise. A waitset diagnostic is\n  1403\t   terminal whatever the entry's `:reason` -- an unsupported reason is by\n  1404\t   definition outside the `#{:next :put}` this check otherwise reads --\n  1405\t   so it is named here rather than falling through to the restore. An\n  1406\t   entry that was never polled carries no status.\"\n  1407\t  [entry]\n  1408\t  (let [status (:status entry)]\n  1409\t    (cond\n  1410\t      (contains? waitset-diagnostics status) status\n  1411\t      (= :link-refused status) status\n  1412\t      (contains? #{:next :put} (:reason entry))\n  1413\t      (when (contains? (get wake-error-outcomes (:reason entry)) status)\n  1414\t        status))))\n  1415\t\n  1416\t\n  1417\t(defn- throw-terminal-resume!\n  1418\t  \"Fail a resumed entry exactly as the immediate operation would have. A\n  1419\t   waitset diagnostic names its status and the entry that earned it: there\n  1420\t   is no stream operation whose error vocabulary it belongs to.\"\n  1421\t  [entry o]\n  1422\t  (cond\n  1423\t    (contains? waitset-diagnostics o)\n  1424\t    (fail \"Wait-set entry woke with a diagnostic\" {:status o, :entry entry})\n  1425\t    ;; a refused or lost link, or a refused install: the effect's error\n  1426\t    (= :link-refused o)\n  1427\t    (fail (str \"Module link refused: \"\n  1428\t               (clojure.core/name (or (:reason (:refusal entry)) :refused)))\n  1429\t          (assoc (:refusal entry) :link-module (:name entry)))\n  1430\t    (= :next (:reason entry))\n  1431\t    (fail \"Stream read failed\"\n  1432\t          {:outcome o,\n  1433\t           :stream-id (:stream-id entry),\n  1434\t           :cursor-id (:id (:cursor-ref entry))})\n  1435\t    :else (fail \"Stream append failed\"\n  1436\t                {:outcome o, :stream-id (:stream-id entry)})))\n  1437\t\n  1438\t\n  1439\t(defn resume-from-run-queue\n  1440\t  \"Pop first entry from the ready-queue, merge its resource updates into\n  1441\t   the private `:resources` table, and restore VM-specific context.\n  1442\t\n  1443\t   Restoration is dispatched here rather than carried on the entry, so a\n  1444\t   ready entry holds registers, ids, and values — never a closure. The\n  1445\t   terminal-outcome check runs before the restore: a woken retry that ended\n  1446\t   in an outcome the immediate operation raises as an error must fail the\n  1447\t   same way here, not reach Yin code as a value.\n  1448\t\n  1449\t   `restore-fn` is called with three arguments, `base entry val`, where\n  1450\t   `val` is the woken `:value` — the same signature `resume-continuation`\n  1451\t   uses, so one restore serves both engine call sites\n  1452\t   (`yin.vm.engine.md` §7, edit 2).\"\n  1453\t  [state restore-fn]\n  1454\t  (let [run-queue (or (:ready-queue state) [])]\n  1455\t    (when (seq run-queue)\n  1456\t      (let [entry (first run-queue)\n  1457\t            rest-queue (subvec run-queue 1)\n  1458\t            updates (:resource-updates entry)\n  1459\t            ;; a woken entry only ever carries engine-minted keyword keys\n  1460\t            ;; (cursor ids)\n  1461\t            _ (when-let [k (some #(when-not (keyword? %) %) (keys updates))]\n  1462\t                (fail \"Ready entry resource update carries a non-minted key\"\n  1463\t                      {:rule :resource-update-key, :key k}))\n  1464\t            base (assoc state\n  1465\t                        :ready-queue rest-queue\n  1466\t                        :resources (merge (:resources state) updates)\n  1467\t                        :blocked? false\n  1468\t                        :halted? false)]\n  1469\t        (if-let [terminal (terminal-resume-outcome entry)]\n  1470\t          (throw-terminal-resume! entry terminal)\n  1471\t          (restore-fn base entry (:value entry)))))))\n  1472\t\n  1473\t\n  1474\t(defn scheduler-round\n  1475\t  \"One round between continuations: poll the wait set, then resume whatever\n  1476\t   woke, through `restore-fn` (`base entry val -> state`, the one restore\n  1477\t   signature `yin.vm.engine.md` §2.1 states). Returns the polled state when\n  1478\t   nothing woke, so a caller always gets a state back.\n  1479\t\n  1480\t   This is `yin.vm.semantic/scheduler-round` with its restore made a\n  1481\t   parameter (`yin.vm.engine.md` §7, edit 1); every VM was writing this\n  1482\t   composition by hand.\"\n  1483\t  [state restore-fn]\n  1484\t  (let [v' (check-wait-set state)]\n  1485\t    (or (resume-from-run-queue v' restore-fn) v')))\n  1486\t\n  1487\t\n  1488\t(defn park-continuation\n  1489\t  \"Add a parked continuation entry and halt the VM.\"\n  1490\t  [state cont-fields]\n  1491\t  (let [id-counter (or (:id-counter state) 0)\n  1492\t        park-id (keyword (str \"parked-\" id-counter))\n  1493\t        parked (merge {:type :parked-continuation, :id park-id} cont-fields)]\n  1494\t    (-> state\n  1495\t        (update :parked assoc park-id parked)\n  1496\t        (assoc :value parked\n  1497\t               :halted? true\n  1498\t               :id-counter (inc id-counter))\n  1499\t        (telemetry/emit-snapshot :park {:parked-id park-id}))))\n  1500\t\n  1501\t\n  1502\t(defn resume-continuation\n  1503\t  \"Restore state from a parked continuation.\"\n  1504\t  [state parked-id resume-val restore-fn]\n  1505\t  (if-let [parked (get-in state [:parked parked-id])]\n  1506\t    (let [new-state (update state :parked dissoc parked-id)]\n  1507\t      (-> (restore-fn new-state parked resume-val)\n  1508\t          (telemetry/emit-snapshot :resume {:parked-id parked-id})))\n  1509\t    (fail \"Cannot resume: parked continuation not found\"\n  1510\t          {:parked-id parked-id})))\n  1511\t\n  1512\t\n  1513\t(defn gensym\n  1514\t  \"Generate a unique ID and return [id updated-state].\"\n  1515\t  ([state] (gensym state \"id\"))\n  1516\t  ([state prefix]\n  1517\t   (let [id-counter (or (:id-counter state) 0)\n  1518\t         id (gen-id prefix id-counter)]\n  1519\t     [id (assoc state :id-counter (inc id-counter))])))\n  1520\t\n  1521\t\n  1522\t(defn handle-stream-block\n  1523\t  [result entry]\n  1524\t  (if (:park result)\n  1525\t    (let [entry (or entry\n  1526\t                    (fail \"Parked entry required for blocking stream\"\n  1527\t                          {:result result}))\n  1528\t          new-state (-> (:state result)\n  1529\t                        (update :wait-set (fnil conj []) entry)\n  1530\t                        (assoc :value :yin/blocked\n  1531\t                               :blocked? true\n  1532\t                               :halted? false))]\n  1533\t      {:state new-state, :value :yin/blocked, :blocked? true})\n  1534\t    {:state (:state result), :value (:value result), :blocked? false}))\n  1535\t\n  1536\t\n  1537\t(defn handle-effect\n  1538\t  \"Dispatch an effect and return {:state updated-state :value v :blocked? bool}.\n  1539\t   park-entry-fns maps :stream/put and :stream/next to functions that build\n  1540\t   wait entries.\n   515\t\n   516\t   `make-stream` is a function of a capacity returning a create outcome. Its\n   517\t   outcomes are ok, invalid-spec, not-found and transport-error; anything but\n   518\t   ok fails, because a half-built VM is worse than a construction error.\"\n   519\t  [make-stream capacity what]\n   520\t  (when-not (fn? make-stream)\n   521\t    (throw (ex-info \"This VM was constructed without :make-stream, so it cannot create streams\"\n   522\t                    {:what what})))\n   523\t  (let [result (make-stream capacity)\n   524\t        outcome (:dao.stream/outcome result)]\n   525\t    (if (= :dao.stream/ok outcome)\n   526\t      (:dao.stream/handle result)\n   527\t      (throw (ex-info \"Stream creation failed\" {:what what, :outcome outcome})))))\n   528\t\n   529\t\n   530\t(defn mint-oldest\n   531\t  \"Mint a cursor at `:dao.stream/oldest` on a handle, or fail.\n   532\t\n   533\t   Every fabricated v1 cursor was semantically absolute position zero. That\n   534\t   equals `:dao.stream/oldest` on a fresh stream but diverges from\n   535\t   `:dao.stream/newest` the moment a stream has history before the cursor\n   536\t   exists — and callers pre-fill streams before constructing a VM, so the\n   537\t   choice is observable. Minting is a stream operation, so it carries\n   538\t   `closed` and `transport-error` outcomes to handle here.\"\n   539\t  [handle what]\n   540\t  (let [result (stream/cursor handle stream/anchor-oldest)\n   541\t        outcome (:dao.stream/outcome result)]\n   542\t    (if (= :dao.stream/ok outcome)\n   543\t      (:dao.stream/cursor result)\n   544\t      (throw (ex-info \"Cursor mint failed\" {:what what, :outcome outcome})))))\n   545\t\n   546\t\n   547\t(defn cursor-entry\n   548\t  \"The VM-internal cursor representation: a stream key and an opaque cursor.\"\n   549\t  [stream-id cursor]\n   550\t  {:stream-id stream-id, :cursor cursor})\n   551\t\n   552\t\n   553\t;; Bytecode Opcodes\n   554\t;; =============================================================================\n   555\t;; Shared instruction set for stack and register bytecode VMs.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
